\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}

\title{Anchoring in Differential Diagnosis:\\A Vignette Study with Resident Physicians}
\author{S.~Aldridge and R.~Nguyen}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Diagnostic errors are a persistent source of preventable harm,
and cognitive biases are among their most studied contributors.
The anchoring bias --- insufficient adjustment from an initial
hypothesis --- is a natural candidate mechanism
\cite{tversky1974}.
Early evidence from case-vignette designs suggests that
residents anchor on the first plausible diagnosis
and underweight disconfirming findings \cite{gilovich2002}.

\section{Methods}
We recruited 84 resident physicians to diagnose six
standardised vignettes.
Half of the participants received a plausible but incorrect
preliminary diagnosis before reading the case;
the remainder saw the same vignettes without a prime.
The primary outcome was whether the final diagnosis matched
the vignette's reference diagnosis.

\section{Results}
Primed residents endorsed the suggested diagnosis in
41\% of cases, against 12\% in the control arm.
Adjustment away from the prime was slower when the
disconfirming evidence appeared late in the vignette.

\section{Discussion}
Anchoring effects in our vignettes are sizeable and
behaviourally consequential.
This pattern is consistent with the broader literature on
heuristics in clinical judgment.
Interventions that force early consideration of alternatives
deserve evaluation in clinical settings.

